Every piece of software I&rsquo;ve developed elicits the same two reactions when users see it for the first time:
First: &ldquo;Wow, this is cool. We can really use this.&rdquo;
Then, immediately: &ldquo;Yes, but&hellip; now that I see it, what about this? Wouldn&rsquo;t it be nice if&hellip;? Whatever happened to&hellip;?&rdquo;
The &ldquo;Yes, But&rdquo; syndrome is universal. It comes from the nature of software as something intangible &ndash; until users see it, they can&rsquo;t fully articulate what they need. Seeing the implementation triggers new ideas, new concerns, new requirements that weren&rsquo;t visible before.
The only defence is involving users early and often, so the &ldquo;but&rdquo; comes before you&rsquo;ve invested too much in the wrong direction.
